        ":question/problem": "What is the value of $\\frac{2}{3}\\int_{e}^{e^{2}}\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{x}{1}\\right)\\operatorname{ln}\\left(\\frac{x}{1}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let's set $u=\\ln\\left(\\left(\\frac{x}{1}\\right)\\right).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\u27f9\\,1\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $e$ | $e^{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\int_{e}^{e^{2}}\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{x}{1}\\right)\\operatorname{ln}\\left(\\frac{x}{1}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ & =\\frac{2}{3}\\int_{e}^{e^{2}}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\\left(\\frac{x}{1}\\right)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{x}{1}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ & =\\frac{2}{3}\\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot\\left((\\operatorname{ln}Abs(u))\\right)|_{1}^{2} \\\\ & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot\\left(\\operatorname{ln}2-\\operatorname{ln}1\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot\\left(\\operatorname{ln}2\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
